course: dictionary
lesson: 11169
title: Windward and the rain shadow
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Windward is a word about direction and wind. The wind blows from one way, and the windward side is the side that faces it, the side the wind blows onto. «Mountains force moist air to rise and drop rain on the windward side» tells you where the rain falls.
explain: Context helps you work out the meaning. The sentence is about moist air and rain, so windward must be about the side where the wind arrives. The side hidden from the wind is the opposite, and that changes how wet it is.
explain: Sailors use the word too. To sail to windward means to sail into the wind, and the windward side of a boat is the side facing the wind. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «the side facing the wind» still makes sense.
fact: Windward means on the side facing the wind.
fact: The windward side of a mountain often gets more rain, because moist air is pushed up, cools and drops its water.
fact: The sheltered side of a mountain, away from the wind, is called the leeward side, and it is often drier.
fact: A dry area behind a mountain is called a rain shadow.
fact: Windward is used in sailing too, as in «to windward», and in place names such as the Windward Islands.
exercise: «Mountains force moist air to rise and drop rain on the windward side.» Which side of the mountain gets the rain? | The side with the most roads | The very top of the peak | The leeward side, the sheltered side | *The windward side, the side facing the wind
exercise: What does windward mean? | *The side facing the wind | The side facing away from the wind | A kind of heavy rain | The strongest wind of the year
exercise: The dry area behind a mountain, where little rain falls, is called what? | A windward island | A storm front | *A rain shadow | A sea breeze
test: «Mountains force moist air to rise and drop rain on the windward side, leaving a dry rain shadow on the other side.» Which side is drier? | Neither side has any wind | *The leeward side, away from the wind | Both sides are equally wet | The windward side, facing the wind
test: A sailor says, «We must sail to windward.» What does this tell you? | *They must sail into the wind | They must sail on a lake only | They must sail away from the wind | They must stop sailing
exam: Why is the windward side of a mountain often wetter than the leeward side? | Because it is always in the sun | Because the wind blows away from it | *Because moist air is pushed up the windward side, cools and drops rain | Because the leeward side has more clouds
